  "Question": "If the altitude of an equilateral triangle is \\(\\sqrt6,\\) then the area is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\sqrt2\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\sqrt3\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\sqrt3\\)",
    "D": "\\(6\\sqrt2\\)",
    "E": "\\(12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "The altitude of an equilateral triangle bisects the opposite side. This creates a right triangle that lets us find the base needed for the area formula.\n\nLet the side length be \\(s>0\\). The right triangle has legs \\(s/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt6\\), and hypotenuse \\(s\\). By the Pythagorean theorem,\n\\[\n\\frac{s^2}{4}+6=s^2,\n\\qquad \\frac{3s^2}{4}=6.\n\\]\nThus \\(s^2=8\\), and positivity gives \\(s=2\\sqrt2\\). The area is therefore\n\\[\n\\frac12\\cdot2\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt6\n=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{2\\sqrt3}\\), choice B.",
